    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-6834",
      "knowledge_point_id": "bbf50e27-a8c3-52e7-be21-5d7ed61e5512",
      "knowledge_point": "Filling a Table of Values for a Parametric Curve",
      "problem": "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-4,\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{t}{2},\\,-1\\le t\\le 5,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $a$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $5$ | $12$ | $21$ |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $b$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $c$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a-b+c.$",
      "worked_solution": "- To find the value of $a,$ we substitute $t=-1$ into $x={t}^{2}-4.$ This gives\n\n$$\na=(-1)^{2}-4=1-4=-3.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $b,$ we substitute $t=1$ into $y=\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{t}{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\nb=\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $c,$ we substitute $t=5$ into $y=\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{t}{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\nc=\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na-b+c=-3-2+0=-5.\n$$",
